llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
124): at one token, the sigmoid gate of a gated attention layer reads its
gate values in place instead of from a CONT copy.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Single-token decode on an RX 590 pays for every dispatch as well as for the
work in it: each one costs about 4.3 us of command-processor time however
small the kernel, a 2.65 us floor plus 1.65 us for the vector-L1 invalidate
that every dispatch needs on gfx8 (docs/FINDINGS.md, "A kernel can read
stale data from the vector L1 across dispatches").

The attention layers of Qwen3.5/3.6 project q and an output gate together
and take the gate as a strided view of the projection (every other row of
head size). The graph copies it out with CONT (cpy_scalar, 4096 floats on
Qwen3.6 35B-A3B) before SIGMOID -> MUL, which the CUDA backend already runs
as one fused kernel. That kernel indexes its input by rows of n values o0
apart, so it can read the view where it is: 10 dispatches per token fewer on
Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (10 attention layers).

Measured on an RX 590 (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, every expert in system RAM,
llama-bench -ncmoe 99, q8_0 KV cache): taken 10 times (once per attention
layer) per decode token, as expected; llama-perplexity at one token per
batch gives per-chunk values and summary lines equal to the build without
the patch, and greedy output (128 tokens) is byte-identical. 123-125
together: tg128 26.33 / 26.26 / 26.31 -> 26.34 / 26.67 / 26.48 tok/s in
three alternating rounds (+0.7%).

WHAT
----
ggml_cuda_match_attn_gate (124) also folds the SIGMOID's input when it is an
f32 CONT, read only by the SIGMOID and within 48 nodes before it, of an f32
view whose elements are contiguous within rows and whose rows are evenly
spaced: the CONT is skipped when the loop reaches it, and the gate reads the
view with n = its row length and o0 = its row stride, through
fwht_sigmoid_mul_f32 when 124's transform is folded too, otherwise through
ggml_cuda_op_sigmoid_mul_strided, which launches
unary_gated_op_kernel<op_sigmoid> with the view as x and the other operand
as a contiguous g. Nothing between the CONT and the SIGMOID may write the
view's bytes, and the MUL's output may not overlap them; graph_optimize
keeps the viewed tensor allocated until the MUL.

Numerics are unchanged: CONT copies f32 to f32 exactly, and every gate
value is op_sigmoid(x) * g of the same x and g in the same kernel code.

